\section{What "Log-Gamma" means here}{
The \strong{log-gamma} distribution is the distribution of \code{log(G)} for a Gamma
variate \code{G}. Used as the distribution of the \emph{log decision time} - in the same
way the Normal is used in the shifted LogNormal - it gives a location-scale
family on the log scale with one extra shape parameter:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{log(RT - ndt) = mu + sigma * w,   w ~ standardized log-gamma with k = 1 / shape^2
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Equivalently, \code{RT - ndt} follows a \strong{generalized gamma} distribution
(Stacy, 1962) in the parameterization of Prentice (1974), i.e.
\code{flexsurv::dgengamma(mu, sigma, Q = shape)}. The two names describe the same
model: "log-gamma" names the distribution of \code{log(RT - ndt)}, "generalized
gamma" names the distribution of \code{RT - ndt} itself.

A \strong{two-parameter} log-gamma is not a usable RT model, which is why there is
a third parameter here. Exponentiating a plain two-parameter Gamma variate
gives support on \verb{(1, Inf)}, so decision times would be forced above one
second; adding a scale to fix that produces a second shift, perfectly
confounded with \code{ndt}; and letting \code{log(RT - ndt)} be log-gamma with no
location or scale just gives back the Gamma. Only the three-parameter
location-scale-shape version is both non-degenerate and closed under a change
of time unit (\code{mu -> mu + log(c)}), which everything else here relies on.
}

\section{Relation to other "log-gamma" implementations}{
The name is used for two different distributions, and only one of them is
this one.

\strong{\code{scipy.stats.loggamma}} is the same distribution. It is \code{log(G)} for
\code{G ~ Gamma(c)}, with the usual \code{loc} and \code{scale}, so it is a three-parameter
family exactly as this one is - \code{c} is the shape parameter, and it is not
optional there either. Taking \code{scipy}'s variate to be \code{log(RT - ndt)}, the
two line up exactly (verified to 3e-15):

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{c = 1 / shape^2      scale = sigma / shape      loc = mu + (sigma / shape) * log(shape^2)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Two deliberate differences. \code{shape} here is standardized so that the Normal
limit sits at \code{shape = 0}, an interior point; in \code{scipy}'s parameterization
that limit is \code{c -> Inf} with \code{loc} and \code{scale} drifting off to compensate,
which is not something a sampler can explore. And because \code{scipy} requires
\code{scale > 0}, it covers only \code{shape > 0}; the \code{shape < 0} half here - the
inverse-Weibull side, with the power-law right tail - is the reflection, and
would need \code{-loggamma} there.

\strong{\code{actuar::dlgamma}} is a \emph{different} distribution: \code{exp(G)} rather than
\code{log(G)}, hence its support of \verb{(1, Inf)}. That is the version with only two
parameters, and the reason it needs only two is also the reason it is no use
for reaction times - see the paragraph above on why exponentiating a Gamma
does not give a usable RT model.
}

\section{\code{shape}, and the families it nests}{
\code{shape} sets the skewness of the log-gamma on the log-RT scale; the Gamma it
is the log of has its own shape \code{k = 1 / shape^2}. Throughout the docs below,
"shape" unqualified means this parameter, never \code{k}.

It is unconstrained, with \code{shape = 0} in the \emph{interior} rather than at a
boundary, which is what makes it usable as a free parameter:\tabular{lll}{
   \code{shape} \tab Distribution \tab right tail \cr
   \verb{< -1} \tab heavier still than the inverse Weibull \tab power law \cr
   \verb{= -1} \tab \strong{inverse Weibull} (Frechet) \tab power law \cr
   \verb{-1 to 0} \tab between the LogNormal and the inverse Weibull \tab power law \cr
   \verb{= 0} \tab \strong{LogNormal} - exactly \code{\link[=cogmod_lognormal]{cogmod_lognormal()}} \tab lognormal \cr
   \verb{0 to 1} \tab between the LogNormal and the Weibull; \strong{Gamma} (shape \code{1 / sigma^2}) at \code{shape = sigma} \tab lighter than lognormal \cr
   \verb{= 1} \tab \strong{Weibull}, shape \code{1 / sigma} \tab lighter than lognormal \cr
   \verb{> 1} \tab lighter still than the Weibull \tab lightest \cr
}


The right tail decays like \code{exp(-c * t^(shape / sigma))} for \code{shape > 0}, so
it thins monotonically as \code{shape} rises, and becomes a power law for
\code{shape < 0}. \code{shape} therefore runs from heavy-tailed at the top of the table
to light-tailed at the bottom, through the LogNormal in the middle. The
Gamma sits inside \verb{0 to 1} for any \code{sigma < 1}, which covers most RT data.

The model is therefore a strict generalisation of the shifted LogNormal, and
fitting it is a way of \emph{testing} whether the LogNormal shape is adequate: an
interval for \code{shape} covering 0 says it is.
}

\section{Where it misbehaves: \code{sigma * shape >= 1}}{
Just above the shift the decision density behaves like a Gamma whose own
shape parameter is \code{1 / (sigma * shape)}. When \code{sigma * shape >= 1} that Gamma
shape falls below 1 and the
density becomes \strong{unbounded at \code{ndt}}, so the likelihood can be driven up
without limit by pushing \code{ndt} toward the fastest response - the exact
pathology the outlier component exists to remove, reintroduced through the
shape parameter. The outlier component cannot repair it, because it adds
density rather than capping it.

This is the same degeneracy the shifted Gamma and shifted Weibull have when
their own shape falls below 1; it is inherited here, not introduced. In
practice the prior on
\code{shape} is what keeps you out of it: \code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} uses \code{normal(0, 0.5)} on
the intercept, which for a typical \code{sigma} around \code{0.5} leaves the boundary
at \code{shape = 2}, four prior SDs away. A posterior for \code{shape} pushing up against
\code{1 / sigma} is the model asking for a spike at the shift, not for a
decision-time distribution.

Negative \code{shape} has the mirror-image caveat: the right tail is a power law, and
the mean of the decision component is finite only when \code{sigma * abs(shape) < 1}.
\code{posterior_epred()} returns \code{Inf} where it is not.
}

\section{Fit with \code{init = 0}}{
The prior keeps the \emph{posterior} clear of that boundary, but it does not
control where a chain \strong{starts}. \code{brms} initialises on the unconstrained
scale from \code{U(-2, 2)}, which for the default links puts \code{shape} in \verb{(-2, 2)} and
\code{sigma} in \verb{(0.13, 2.13)} - and about \strong{15\% of chains start with
\code{sigma * shape >= 1}}. A chain starting inside the unbounded region falls into
the spike at \code{ndt} and does not come back out: it does not error, it simply
runs for as long as you let it while the others finish.

\code{init = 0} removes the problem by construction, starting every chain at
\code{shape = 0} - the LogNormal - with \code{sigma * shape = 0}:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{f <- brms::bf(RT ~ 1, sigma ~ 1, shape ~ 1, ndt ~ 1, poutlier ~ 1,
              family = cogmod_loggamma())
brms::brm(f, data = df,
          prior = cogmod_priors(f, df),
          stanvars = cogmod_stanvars(f),
          init = 0)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

This is not a tuning suggestion to try if sampling looks bad; it is how the
model should be fitted. The one visible symptom of getting it wrong is a
chain that never finishes.
}

\section{\code{ndt} and \code{poutlier}}{
Identical in meaning, parameterization and defaults to \code{\link[=cogmod_lognormal]{cogmod_lognormal()}} - see
its Details for the full account of why \code{ndt} is expressed directly in
seconds, what the half Normal outlier component is for, why its scale is a
constant rather than a \code{dpar}, and why predictions exclude the outlier
component by default. \code{\link[=with_outliers]{with_outliers()}}, \code{\link[=without_outliers]{without_outliers()}}, \code{\link[=p_outlier]{p_outlier()}}
and \code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} all work on this family too.
}

\examples{
# shape = 0 is exactly the shifted LogNormal
dcogmod_loggamma(0.9, mu = -0.7, sigma = 0.5, shape = 0, ndt = 0.3)
dcogmod_lognormal(0.9, mu = -0.7, sigma = 0.5, ndt = 0.3)

# Simulate 1000 RTs with 2\% outliers and a slightly Gamma-like shape
rts <- rcogmod_loggamma(1000,
  mu = -0.7, sigma = 0.5, shape = 0.5, ndt = 0.3,
  poutlier = 0.02
)
hist(rts, breaks = 100, xlab = "RT (s)")

# Responses faster than ndt keep positive density, as in cogmod_lognormal()
dcogmod_loggamma(0.1, ndt = 0.3, poutlier = 0.02)
dcogmod_loggamma(0.1, ndt = 0.3, poutlier = 0)

# shape = sigma is the shifted Gamma, with shape 1 / sigma^2
dcogmod_loggamma(0.9, mu = -0.7, sigma = 0.5, shape = 0.5, ndt = 0.3)
stats::dgamma(0.6, shape = 4, scale = exp(-0.7) * 0.25)

}
